1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,435
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons! Last time, you learned the fifteen best opening moves.

2
00:00:07,135 --> 00:00:12,359
Today, we're tackling the most powerful piece on the board: the doubling cube.

3
00:00:12,956 --> 00:00:17,940
Knowing when to double and when to take matters just as much as moving your checkers well.

4
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:22,052
By the end of this lesson, you'll make these decisions like a real club player.

5
00:00:26,761 --> 00:00:33,471
The doubling cube is that big die marked two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two and sixty-four.

6
00:00:33,171 --> 00:00:35,926
Players often just call it the cube.

7
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:43,972
It was invented in the clubs of New York, in the nineteen-twenties. And it completely changed the game.

8
00:00:46,398 --> 00:00:50,907
Before the cube, a game that was going badly was played to the very end.

9
00:00:50,607 --> 00:00:53,654
With it, every turn can become a real decision.

10
00:00:56,718 --> 00:01:01,313
At the start of the game, the game is worth one, and the cube sits in the middle.

11
00:01:01,013 --> 00:01:02,585
Both players can use it.

12
00:01:04,123 --> 00:01:07,960
On your turn, before rolling the dice, you can offer to double.

13
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:11,497
Careful: once the dice are rolled, it's too late for that turn.

14
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:19,173
Your opponent then has two choices. If they pass, they give up the game and lose its current value.

15
00:01:20,377 --> 00:01:24,844
If they take, the game's value doubles, and the cube goes to their side.

16
00:01:24,544 --> 00:01:29,011
From then on, only they can redouble later, if the game turns their way.

17
00:01:30,946 --> 00:01:35,697
And if they redouble, the game is worth four, and the cube comes back to you.

18
00:01:35,397 --> 00:01:39,166
So it travels back and forth, every time luck changes sides.

19
00:01:41,591 --> 00:01:47,392
The cube doesn't only multiply a single win. It also multiplies gammons and backgammons.

20
00:01:47,916 --> 00:01:52,496
A gammon is worth two points. With the cube on four, it's worth eight!

21
00:01:52,712 --> 00:01:57,587
And a backgammon, worth three points, is worth twelve with the cube on four.

22
00:01:57,287 --> 00:02:00,597
That's why a single game can decide a whole match.

23
00:02:02,977 --> 00:02:08,448
Now for the most important calculation in backgammon: the twenty-five percent rule.

24
00:02:08,148 --> 00:02:10,629
It tells you when to take a double.

25
00:02:11,412 --> 00:02:15,367
Let's say the game is worth one, and your opponent offers you a double.

26
00:02:16,384 --> 00:02:19,651
If you pass, you lose one point, for sure.

27
00:02:19,351 --> 00:02:25,233
If you take, the game is worth two: you'll either lose two points or score two.

28
00:02:25,601 --> 00:02:30,905
Picture four identical games, where you come out on top once in four.

29
00:02:30,605 --> 00:02:37,650
By taking, you lose two points three times, that's six, and you score two. Total: minus four.

30
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:44,725
By passing all four times, you lose one point four times. Total: minus four as well.

31
00:02:44,425 --> 00:02:49,542
So at twenty-five percent, taking or passing comes to the same thing.

32
00:02:50,242 --> 00:02:55,241
The owl's tip: if you win at least one game in four from this position, take!

33
00:02:54,941 --> 00:03:01,344
And since you then own the cube, you can turn it again if luck changes. That's a little extra bonus.

34
00:03:04,735 --> 00:03:07,948
Now let's switch sides: when should you double?

35
00:03:08,098 --> 00:03:12,354
If you double too early, you hand the cube to your opponent for nothing.

36
00:03:12,054 --> 00:03:14,222
They can use it against you later.

37
00:03:15,617 --> 00:03:22,880
If you double too late, your position has become so strong that your opponent passes, and you only score a single point.

38
00:03:23,144 --> 00:03:29,558
The right moment is when your position is clearly better, and it might become too good by your next turn.

39
00:03:29,258 --> 00:03:31,946
Players say you mustn't lose your market.

40
00:03:35,857 --> 00:03:39,137
Let's look at the situations where people double most often.

41
00:03:39,287 --> 00:03:40,507
First, the race.

42
00:03:40,207 --> 00:03:48,958
When all that's left is bringing your checkers home and bearing off, you count pips: the total number of points your checkers still have to travel.

43
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:56,435
At the end of the game, you usually double with about an eight percent lead, and you take with up to about twelve percent behind.

44
00:03:56,135 --> 00:03:59,389
We'll look at the pip count in detail in the next lesson.

45
00:04:00,551 --> 00:04:01,826
Next, the attack.

46
00:04:01,526 --> 00:04:10,089
If several of your opponent's checkers are within hitting range and your home board is strong, double before the dice give you the decisive hit.

47
00:04:10,856 --> 00:04:12,822
And finally, the blockade.

48
00:04:12,522 --> 00:04:19,229
A solid prime, that six-point wall, behind one of your opponent's checkers often justifies a double.

49
00:04:19,443 --> 00:04:25,106
But watch out for the trap: if you have big chances of winning a gammon, don't double!

50
00:04:24,806 --> 00:04:30,593
Keep playing to score two points instead of one. That's called being too good to double.

51
00:04:34,429 --> 00:04:37,016
There are also a few special rules.

52
00:04:37,578 --> 00:04:42,068
In a match, for example to seven points, the Crawford rule applies.

53
00:04:41,768 --> 00:04:47,759
When one player gets within one point of victory, the next game is played without the cube.

54
00:04:47,459 --> 00:04:49,760
After that, the cube comes back.

55
00:04:50,372 --> 00:04:55,177
This rule stops the trailing player from doubling straight away, with nothing to lose.

56
00:04:55,631 --> 00:05:00,014
In free play, outside a match, two optional rules are common.

57
00:04:59,714 --> 00:05:05,368
The Jacoby rule: a gammon or a backgammon only counts if the cube has been used.

58
00:05:06,146 --> 00:05:12,745
And the beaver: a player who receives a double can redouble immediately, while keeping the cube on their side.

59
00:05:14,645 --> 00:05:18,964
To finish, here are the most common beginner mistakes.

60
00:05:19,314 --> 00:05:24,466
The first: passing doubles you should have taken. Beginners give up far too often!

61
00:05:24,166 --> 00:05:26,773
Remember: one chance in four is enough.

62
00:05:29,638 --> 00:05:31,596
The second: never doubling.

63
00:05:31,296 --> 00:05:37,429
You end up scoring one point where your opponent would have given up much earlier… or lost two.

64
00:05:38,524 --> 00:05:44,904
The third: forgetting about gammons. A position where you risk being gammoned is easier to pass.

65
00:05:46,151 --> 00:05:50,175
To judge your chances well, it helps to know the dice probabilities.

66
00:05:49,875 --> 00:05:55,048
And on TickGammon, the analysis of your games shows you every questionable cube decision.

67
00:05:59,027 --> 00:06:02,554
And there you go, you now know how to use the doubling cube!

68
00:06:02,254 --> 00:06:07,986
You'll find the twenty-five percent rule and all these tips on the Doubling cube page, on TickGammon.

69
00:06:09,065 --> 00:06:15,951
To practise, play for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

70
00:06:15,651 --> 00:06:21,005
Next time, I'll teach you about the race and bearing off. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

71
00:06:20,705 --> 00:06:22,708
See you soon around the board!

